meta {
  name: 06 WebSocket Events
  type: ws
  seq: 6
}

ws {
  url: {{ws_url}}
}

body:ws {
  message connect [text] {
    40{"token":"{{token}}"}
  }

  message pong [text] {
    3
  }
}

docs {
  # Socket.IO events

  Connect and wait for the Engine.IO packet `0{...}`.
  Send `connect` to authenticate. A valid token returns `40{"sid":"..."}`.
  Send `pong` when the server sends `2`, or the connection closes.
  Then run `02-Create-Job`.

  The raw event frames start with `42`. The status order is `queued`,
  `scraping`, `running`, then `done` or `failed`. `scraping` comes from the
  scraper. `running` starts when analysis begins. A missing LLM key ends in
  `failed`, not `done`. The `job:update` frame repeats every status.

  # Independent socket demo

  Keep `auth-svc`, `redis`, `gateway-svc`, and `nginx` running.
  The scraper and analysis can stay stopped.

  Use a job id from the same organization as the token. Publish the same
  channel that the workers use:

  ```bash
  docker compose exec -T redis redis-cli PUBLISH job-events \
    '{"job_id":"JOB_ID","status":"running"}'

  docker compose exec -T redis redis-cli PUBLISH job-events \
    '{"job_id":"JOB_ID","status":"done"}'
  ```

  Bruno shows `job:running`, `job:update`, `job:done`, and `job:update`.
  This is a transport demo with a synthetic event. The gateway still
  validates the token and sends only to the organization room.
}
